| Reference providers (store, reward, badge) | agentic-portal `bundles/features/player-engagement-offers/microservices/PlayerEngagement{CampaignOffer,RewardOffer,BadgeOffer}Service/` |
| Web client | BeamableProduct `web/src/services/OffersService.ts` |

## Business Goals Constraints

- An offer is something a player buys with soft currency, or is simply given. Real-money offers need product ids, a native purchase flow and receipts; they are not this federation.
- Beamable's offer registry owns every grant's state. A provider resolves, delivers and undoes; it never stores whether a grant exists, is visible or was taken.
- A grant becomes visible only when whatever tells the player about it is durable (a campaign's staged message, an operator's answered request). A grant whose message never went out is never shown.
- The registry knows each grant's provider, so the player lists every provider's offers in one call and redeems by grant id alone.
- Beamable ships three providers (`beamable_virtual_store`, `beamable_reward`, `beamable_badge`); a game's own provider registers under its own federation id and is treated identically. Nothing branches on the id.

## Performance Constraints

- **Grant calls**: chunks of `Campaigns:Send:MaxGrantsPerCall` (250, at most 1000) grants per `GrantOffers`, up to `MaxParallelGrants` (48) calls in flight per provider from each campaign runtime (fewer when the provider answers `maxInFlight`, split among the runtimes), `OfferGroupGrantTimeoutMs` (30 s) per call. A send page holds up to `MaxGrantsPerPage` (10,000) grants. Operator grants: one grant per call, `Offer:Gateway:GrantTimeoutMs` (60 s).
- **Pacing is the provider's**: there is no rate setting for a provider. Grant and revoke answers carry `maxInFlight` (calls you take at once; absent = Beamable climbs back to its ceiling one call a second) and `retryAfterMs` for the provider as a whole, and `throttled` (+ optional `retryAfterMs`) per item; a throttled item goes back to the queue without counting as a failure. The platform only keeps its own ceilings.
- **Redeem / availability / revoke calls**: 30 s federation call timeout. The list waits `Offer:Gateway:AvailabilityTimeoutMs` (5 s) per provider, then lists that provider's grants without its rules.
- **List**: `Offer:Service:PlayerGrantsPageSize` (512) grants per page, one `GetOffers` call per provider present on the page, except providers whose realm config sets `hasAvailabilityRules` to `false` (answered available without a call).
- **Provider discovery**: a provider's registration is kept `Federation:OfferDiscoveryCacheMs` (5 s) per realm, and dropped when a call through it fails. Concurrent calls share one in-flight lookup.
- **Claim lease**: `Offer:Service:ClaimLeaseSeconds` (120). A player can retry an ambiguous redeem after it.
- **Redelivery**: an unanswered delivery is asked again by the platform, with the redeem's params, `Offer:Service:RedeliveryRetryBaseSeconds` (20) after it, doubling up to `RedeliveryRetryMaxIntervalSeconds` (3600), until the grant's window ends.
- **Revokes**: always through the revoke worker's queue (an operator's revoke and a forfeit are due at once), up to `Offer:Workers:MaxRevokesPerCall` (256) grants per call, at most `RevokeConcurrency` (16) calls in flight and `RevokeCallsPerSecond` (200), lower when the provider asks.
- **Metadata**: `Offer:Service:MaxMetadataBytes` (16 KiB, keys + values, UTF-8) per grant.
- **Player rate limits** (per player being acted on): redeem 10/s burst 20, read 10/s burst 20 (`Offer:Gateway:Redeem*`, `Read*`). Request maps: at most 256 entries, values up to 8,192 chars.
- **Expiry**: campaign grants last the offer's `durationMs` or `Campaigns:Send:DefaultOfferDurationMs` (30 days); operator grants default to `Offer:Gateway:DefaultGrantDurationSeconds` (30 days).

## Usage Guide

### 1. Create the service

```
beam_exec("project new service <Name>OfferService -q")
```
{{ if commands["project new service"] }}
#### `beam project new service` options
| Option | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
{{ for opt in commands["project new service"].options }}`{{ opt.name }}` | {{ opt.type }} | {{ opt.description }}
{{ end }}{{ end }}

A provider needs no storage: whether a delivery may be asked for again is realm config (step 2), and the registry decides. It may still keep state of its own under each `grantId`, up to managing the whole grant in a storage of its own, with no service outside it.

### 2. Federation id

```csharp
[FederationId(Id)]
public class MyShopId : IFederationId
{
    public const string Id = "my_shop";
}
```

Starts with a letter; letters, digits, `_` only. Campaign send nodes and the registry address the provider by it.

How Beamable treats the provider is realm config, not code: each setting is `offers|{federationId}.<setting>`, set on the realm, its zone or the customer (most specific wins). A changed value holds from the next call; what a grant takes from it (revoke lead, redelivery window, ambiguous outcome) is fixed on the grant when it is written.

| Realm config | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `offers\|my_shop.hasAvailabilityRules` | `true` | `false`: the list answers every grant available without calling `GetOffers` |
| `offers\|my_shop.revokeLeadSeconds` | `0` | Grants end, and are revoked, this long before their expiry (capped by the platform) |
| `offers\|my_shop.ambiguousRedeliverySeconds` | 12 h | How long an unanswered delivery may be asked for again (step 5); `0` never asks again |
| `offers\|my_shop.ambiguousOutcome` | `NotDelivered` | What an unanswered delivery is taken to have been past that window (step 5) |
| `offers\|my_shop.maxGrantsInBatch` | 250 | Most grants per `GrantOffers` call, at most 1000 |
| `offers\|my_shop.maxRevokesInBatch` | 256 | Most grants per `RevokeOffer` call, at most 1000 |

A value that does not read as its type takes the default and logs a warning (at most one a minute per realm, provider and setting).

### 3. Implement all four methods

```csharp
[Microservice("MyShopOfferService")]
public class MyShopOfferService : Microservice, IFederatedVirtualOffer<MyShopId>
{
    async Promise<OfferGrantBatchResponse> IFederatedVirtualOffer<MyShopId>.GrantOffers(
        List<OfferGrantItem> grants) { /* step 4 */ }

    async Promise<OfferRedeemResponse> IFederatedVirtualOffer<MyShopId>.RedeemOffer(
        OfferGrantRef grant, Dictionary<string, string> @params) { /* step 5 */ }

    async Promise<OfferRevokeBatchResponse> IFederatedVirtualOffer<MyShopId>.RevokeOffer(
        List<OfferGrantRef> revokes) { /* step 6 */ }

    async Promise<List<OfferAvailability>> IFederatedVirtualOffer<MyShopId>.GetOffers(
        List<OfferGrantRef> grants) { /* step 6 */ }
}
```

- Parameter names are the body fields the platform posts (`grants`, `grant` + `params`, `revokes`, `grants`); keep them.
- Routes are the literal method names: `{federationId}/GrantOffers`, `RedeemOffer`, `RevokeOffer`, `GetOffers`. No `[Callable]` on these methods.
- `Program.cs` is stock: `BeamServer.Create().IncludeRoutes<MyShopOfferService>(routePrefix: "").RunForever()`.

### 4. GrantOffers: resolve, never record

Answer an `OfferGrantBatchResponse` whose `grants` hold exactly one `OfferGrantResponse` per item, in the same order.

| `OfferGrantItem` field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `grantId` | Registry id; idempotency key for everything about this grant |
| `playerId`, `offerId` | Who, and the provider's own opaque offer reference |
| `expiresAtUnixSeconds` | When the grant stops being redeemable; hand it to a third-party service if the offer lives there |
| `metadata` | Caller data: authored fields (`offer_*` by convention) plus platform attribution keys (`beam_campaign`, `beam_campaign_version`, `beam_node`, `beam_outreach`). Ignore `beam_*` keys when reading authored fields |

```csharp
new OfferGrantResponse
{
    grantId = item.grantId,
    success = true,
    offer = new OfferItem { offerId = item.offerId, title = "Starter pack", cost = { ... }, rewards = { ... } },
    providerMetadata = { ["sku"] = "abc" },   // handed back on redeem and revoke
};
new OfferGrantResponse { grantId = item.grantId, success = false,
    status = OfferContract.UnavailableStatus, message = "sold out", retriable = false };
```

- `offer` is required on success: it is stored and shown to the player, so later catalog edits do not change what was granted.
- `retriable = true` holds the campaign's send and asks again with the same grant ids; `false` sends the message without the offer. A failed or timed-out call is always retried.
- A refused member of an `Exclusive` group fails the whole group for that player.
- The same grant id may arrive again (retries, a lost answer): answer it as the grant it already is.
- Hit a limit (yours, or a service you call answered 429)? Answer the grants you did not attempt `throttled = true` (status `OfferContract.OverCapacityStatus`), with `retryAfterMs` when one grant must wait on its own. Set `maxInFlight` on the batch to how many calls you take at once (repeat it on every answer while the limit holds), or `retryAfterMs` on the batch to get none for a while. How many grants one call carries is `maxGrantsInBatch` / `maxRevokesInBatch` in your settings. None of it counts as a failure.

```csharp
return new OfferGrantBatchResponse
{
    grants = answers,          // the throttled ones included, in order
    retryAfterMs = 60_000,     // the service you call said Retry-After: 60
};
```

### 5. RedeemOffer: deliver once

The registry has already claimed the grant for this redeem and checked the campaign's gate; enforce only your own rules.

| `OfferRedeemOutcome` | Registry effect | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `Settled` | Redeemed; exclusive siblings and named forfeits revoked | Delivered, or nothing to deliver |
| `Refused` | Back to Granted, player can retry | Definitely nothing moved (a 4xx other than 408) |
| `Ambiguous` | Stays Claiming; asked again on the redelivery curve (20 s, doubling) | Cannot tell (timeout, 5xx, dropped connection) |

- The same grant id can be redeemed again after `Ambiguous` or a failed call: the repeat must be the same delivery. Key the delivery on `grantId` (an idempotency key downstream). If your service deduplicates a repeat only for a while, set the realm config `offers|{federationId}.ambiguousRedeliverySeconds` to that window (unset = the platform default (12 h); 0 = never asked again): past it the registry stops asking.
- If a repeat is not safe at all (a purchase with no idempotency key downstream), set `offers|{federationId}.ambiguousOutcome` to `Hold`. With `NotDelivered`, a delivery that went through without an answer is given back and the player can be delivered, and charged, twice.
- `OfferGrantRef` carries `offer`, `metadata` and `providerMetadata` exactly as stored at grant time; read the definition from them rather than from a catalog that may have changed.
- `outcome` travels by name; `status` and `message` are shown to the player.

Past the window the registry settles the redeem by `offers|{federationId}.ambiguousOutcome`, applied by its expiry worker without waiting for the player:

| `ambiguousOutcome` | Registry effect | Risk |
|---|---|---|
| `NotDelivered` (default) | Back to Granted; the next redeem is a new delivery | Delivered twice if the first one happened |
| `Delivered` | Redeemed; group forfeited | Nothing delivered if the first one did not happen |
| `Hold` | Stays Claiming, listed by `GET /api/offers/awaiting-operator` until `POST /api/offers/resolve { grantId, delivered }` | Needs someone to check your service |

The value is read by name, ignoring case. The reference providers need no redelivery setting: reward's inventory transaction dedup covers the 12 h default; store and badge take it too.

### 6. RevokeOffer and GetOffers

- `RevokeOffer`: undo what grants that ended unredeemed created in your service. Called for every provider; answer `success = true` when there is nothing to undo. Must work from `grantId` alone: a grant whose grant answer was lost has no `offer` and no `providerMetadata`. Answered as an `OfferRevokeBatchResponse` (`revokes`), paced the same way as grants: `throttled` per grant, `maxInFlight` / `retryAfterMs` for the batch. Revokes wait while the customer is sending.
- `GetOffers`: one `OfferAvailability { grantId, available, unavailableReasons }` per grant, by your own rules (stock, schedule, limits). The campaign gate is added by the platform. A provider without rules of its own answers every grant available, and its realm config sets `hasAvailabilityRules` to `false` (step 2) so it is not called.

### 7. Player routes

| Route | Body / query | Answer |
|---|---|---|
| `GET /api/offers` | `playerId`, `states?` (repeated or comma-separated `OfferGrantState`), `federationId?`, `cursor?` | `PlayerOffersPage { offers[], next? }` |
| `POST /api/offers/redeem` | `{ playerId, grantId, params? }` | `RedeemOfferResult { grantId, success, status, message, state, reason? }` |

- Default `states`: `Granted` and `Claiming`. `Pending` and `Failed` are never listed. A grant past its expiry, or of a closed origin, reads `Expired`.
- `PlayerOffer { grantId, federationId, offerId, state, grantedAtUnixSeconds, expiresAtUnixSeconds, offer, available, unavailableReasons[] }`.
- A player may act only on their own grants; non-guest tokens may act on any player. 429 past the rate limit.
- Redeem answers 200 for refusals: `success = false` with `status` `unknown-grant`, `unavailable` (with `reason` when the gate is closed), or `pending` (retry later). A redeemed grant answers `success = true` again.

### 8. Operator routes (`CampaignOffer` resource, Write permission)

| Route | Body | Answer |
|---|---|---|
| `POST /api/offers/grant` | `{ federationId, playerId, offerId, idempotencyKey, originId?, expiresAtUnixSeconds?, metadata? }` | `GrantOfferResult { grantId, success, status, message, retriable }` |
| `POST /api/offers/revoke` | `{ grantId }` | `RevokeOfferResult { grantId, revoked }` |
| `POST /api/offers/revoke-origin` | `{ originId }` | `RevokeOfferOriginResult { originId, closed }` |

- `originId` names a batch (default `manual`); `revoke-origin` expires that batch's open grants in the request's realm. `idempotencyKey` is required: the same key lands on the same grant, so a grant whose answer was lost is retried by repeating it. A grant repeated after it ended stays ended (`grant-ended`) and the provider is not asked again. `expiresAtUnixSeconds`, when given, must be in the future.
- `revoke` ends a Granted grant; one being redeemed finishes its redeem and comes back expired if refused.

### 9. Run and verify

```
beam_exec("project run --ids <Name>OfferService")
```

The OpenAPI document must contain `<federationId>/GrantOffers`, `RedeemOffer`, `RevokeOffer`, `GetOffers`. Publishing a campaign whose offer names a federation with no registered provider fails with `OfferFederationNotRegistered`.
